---
// pitomd.com/guides/youtube — connect a Google Cloud sign-in to Pito
// Studio for channel/video analytics and direct uploads. Linked verbatim
// from Studio's own setup wizard as "pitomd.com/guides/youtube"
// (internal/ui/studio_cockpit_wizard_render.go, wizPhYouTubeCreds).
//
// Written for a non-technical reader (owner: "the canonical reader is my
// wife") — "OAuth", "device-code flow" and "quota units" are named once
// each, in a technically-minded aside at the end of "How it's handled",
// never in the lead sentence of a section.
//
// SOURCE OF TRUTH: pito-tui's README.md ("Connecting a YouTube channel")
// for the live device-code sign-in flow (`y` on the Analytics surface,
// connection stored locally, refreshed automatically, never logged, only
// the masked channel name shown back) and internal/studio/youtube/setup.go's
// SetupSteps for the underlying Google Cloud console flow — that file's
// SetupSteps() is unwired dead code as of this writing (nothing in
// internal/ui calls it), but the flow itself is standard Google Cloud
// setup and the copy below draws on the same steps it lays out. Re-check
// against that file if Google's console changes the exact page names.

import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const TITLE = "Connect YouTube — Pito Studio guides";
const DESCRIPTION =
  "How to connect a YouTube channel to Pito Studio for channel analytics and direct uploads — what you need, why it's useful, and how it's kept private.";

const pageUrl = new URL("/guides/youtube/", Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "HowTo",
      name: TITLE,
      url: pageUrl,
      description: DESCRIPTION,
    },
  ],
};
---

<GuideLayout
  title={TITLE}
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  eyebrow="Analytics & uploads · YouTube"
  heading="Connect YouTube."
  dek="Connecting your channel unlocks real numbers inside Studio and lets you send a finished video straight to YouTube. You'll set this up on Google's own site — Studio never gets a shared or borrowed account, only yours."
  slug="youtube"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section id="need">
    <h2>What you need</h2>
    <ul class="guide-checklist">
      <li>
        <strong>A Google account</strong> that owns or manages the YouTube channel
        you want to connect.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Access to Google's Cloud website</strong> — it's free, and you won't
        need a credit card for this.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>About ten minutes.</strong> You'll only need to do this once per channel.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why Studio wants this</h2>
    <p>
      Two things unlock at once: real numbers for your channel and videos right
      inside Studio — views, how long people watch, which day does best — and,
      if you want it, sending a finished video straight to YouTube from Studio,
      title and all, without leaving the app.
    </p>
    <p>
      Skip this and Studio's editing works exactly the same either way; you'd
      just export the finished file and upload it yourself, the normal way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="handled">
    <h2>How it's handled</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout">
      <p>
        The two codes you paste in stay on this computer. When you connect a
        channel, Studio shows you a short code and a web address — you open that
        address on any device you're already signed into, type the code, and
        Google confirms it's really you. From then on, Studio talks to YouTube
        directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Studio doesn't print or log that connection anywhere — afterward, all
        you see is your channel's own name, with most of it hidden, the same way
        Studio hides a licence key. You can disconnect any time from your Google
        Account's security settings, or by deleting the small project you'll
        create in the steps below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technically minded: this is Google's own OAuth device-code sign-in; the
        codes and the resulting connection are kept in Studio's <code
          >config.toml</code
        > on this machine, and the connection itself refreshes on its own as it nears
        expiry.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="steps">
    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol class="guide-steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Sign in to Google Cloud</h3>
        <p>
          Open the <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/"
            >Google Cloud console</a
          > and sign in with the Google account that owns your YouTube channel. It's
          free, and this step doesn't sign you up for anything — it's just where Google
          keeps the settings that let a program talk to your account.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Create a project</h3>
        <p>
          Near the top of the page there's a project switcher — open it, choose <a
            href="https://console.cloud.google.com/projectcreate">New Project</a
          >, give it any name (<code>Pito</code> is fine), and create it. Wait for
          the switcher to show your new project before moving on.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Turn on the YouTube Data API</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a
            href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/youtube.googleapis.com"
            >this page</a
          >, make sure your new project is selected at the top, and press
          Enable. This is the switch that lets Studio read or upload on your
          behalf — it isn't billed.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Fill in the consent screen</h3>
        <p>
          Google wants to know who's asking for access. On <a
            href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials/consent"
            >this page</a
          >, choose External, fill in your own name and email where it asks, and
          save. Then find "Test users" and add your own Google address — while
          the app is still new, only the accounts you list here can sign in,
          which is exactly what you want, since the only account that will ever
          use it is yours.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Create the sign-in codes</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href="https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials"
            >Credentials</a
          >, press Create Credentials, choose OAuth client ID, then Desktop app
          as the type. Google shows you two codes — leave that page open.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Paste them into Studio</h3>
        <p>
          Copy both codes into Studio's setup wizard — or later, from Settings.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Connect a channel</h3>
        <p>
          Back in Studio, press <span class="guide-key">y</span> on the Analytics
          screen. Studio shows a web address and a short code — open the address on
          any signed-in device, type the code, and Studio waits for Google to confirm
          it. That's the one-time sign-in; Studio won't ask again.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Good to know</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--limits">
      <p>
        <strong>A warning that says "unverified" is normal.</strong> Because this
        is your own small app that nobody has reviewed, Google will warn you about
        it during sign-in. Choose Advanced, then continue anyway — that's expected,
        and safe: it's your own codes talking to your own account.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Only the accounts you listed can sign in, for now.</strong> That's
        fine for a single channel, but if you ever want other accounts to sign in
        too, Google's review process can take a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>There's a daily limit, but it's generous.</strong> Google gives every
        project a daily allowance, and sending one video uses a chunk of it — roughly
        six uploads a day on the free amount. Because this project is yours alone,
        that allowance is entirely yours too.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>It works right away.</strong> Once connected, both the numbers and
        uploads work immediately for that channel — there's no extra approval step
        on Studio's side.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="done">
    <h2>Back in Studio</h2>
    <div class="guide-callout guide-callout--done">
      <span class="guide-callout__tag">Last step</span>
      <p>
        Once the channel shows as connected, the Analytics screen fills in with
        real numbers, and pressing <span class="guide-key">u</span> on a finished
        project sends it straight to YouTube.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
